comments.body:
@reporter yeah, that actually makes me realize this may not be limited to this particular repo.

@participant6 could this issue also affect the overall synchronization time on our CachyOS infrastructure? We use Syncthing to sync our repository mirrors and some other stuff, so I wonder whether this regression could affect our sync performance as well.

The benchmarks suggest that most of the delay can happen during scanning, before Syncthing even starts propagating the changes. If any of our synced folders contain many content-identical files or generated Git data, it might be worth checking the scan duration and whether `findRename` or `IsDeleted` appears in a profile.

@participant1 One more thought after reproducing this: maybe it would be useful to add a small scanner performance regression suite, so similar changes do not silently turn roughly linear scan work into quadratic work again.

The most important synthetic test could use `N = 100, 200, 400` identical, non-empty small files. I would use something like the real 2-byte and 41-byte files from this case, since empty files do not exercise the same rename-detection path.

After the initial indexing, the test could run these cases separately:

* touch the mtimes of all existing files and rescan;
* add another group of content-identical files without deleting anything;
* delete one file and add an identical file under a new name;
* perform many renames while other files with the same `BlocksHash` still exist;
* rename to a new destination and rename over an existing destination.

Besides checking the final index, it would be good to count `Lstat` / `IsDeleted` calls and database candidates visited. Wall-clock time is useful too, but it is much noisier. The operation counts from this issue were deterministic and clearly showed the difference between linear and quadratic behavior.

With #10819 specifically, I think it is important to keep “update existing duplicate files” and “add new duplicate files” as separate tests. The proposed change skips rename detection for updates, but newly appearing files still go through `findRename`.

For a larger pre-release benchmark, perhaps the RC could also be compared with the previous stable release using a few representative workloads:

* fresh scan, unchanged rescan and small incremental scan;
* many unique small files versus many identical small files;
* mass additions, deletions and renames;
* wide and deeply nested directory trees;
* a generated Git/package-build tree containing `.git/subtree-cache` and similar data;
* restart or database upgrade followed by a scan;
* full scan, subdirectory scan and watcher-triggered scan;
* end-to-end synchronization between two devices.

I do not think every possible combination needs to run for every PR. A small deterministic complexity test could run in normal CI, while the larger workloads could run nightly or for release candidates. Comparing filesystem operations, database work, CPU, memory, hashed bytes and time-to-idle against the previous release should make regressions like this much easier to notice before publishing a release.

> I don't think it's a regression, I think it's how it's always been (within recent memory) and triggered by having many content-identical files. Which is something we could probably handle a bit smarter, so leaving this open for improvement.

@participant1 Perhaps next time you are a bit more open to regression reports coming from long-term syncthing users 🙏 I feel that without @participant2's help and benchmarks here, my issue would have been ignored, even though it was indeed a recent regression from some recent changes you made.

@participant3 Thanks for the quick fix! Looking forward for a new release containing the change. I'll remove the exclude pattern after the update ;)
